On 3/7/13 6:27 PM, Jeffrey Arnold wrote:
> One way to implement this in Stan would be to define a new datatype for a vector which is in the AR stationarity and
> invertibility region. The function ARtransPars in the R package dlm maps between the reals and AR parameters using the
> method defined the following paper,
>
> Jones, 1987. Randomly choosing parameters from the stationarity and invertibility region of autoregressive-moving

> average models. /Applied Statistics/, 36.

>
> On Thu, Mar 7, 2013 at 3:19 PM, Carlos <car...@montesgaldon.com <mailto:car...@montesgaldon.com>> wrote:
>
>         Funny you should bring that up.  I've had it on our
>         to-do list for a while.  When I asked Andrew, he suggested
>         it would be a bad idea to do this because the data should
>         constrain the solution to be stationary if the process
>         actually is stationary and you don't want to constrain it to
>         a stationary solution if it's not.
>
>
>     Well, I think that Andrew has a very good point  here :D But... at least my experience is that when you have highly
>     autocorrelated processes (let's say, an AR(1) model with phi=0.9), if I put a non-constrained prior, and I allow the
>     support to be R, even if in general the chain of the parameter is inside (-1,1), sometimes there are outliers such
>     that abs(phi)>1. If we want to focus on means, ok, that is not a problem. But if we want to compute impulse response
>     functions, and want to extract the quantiles by simulation, sometimes the paths explode, and I think that might be
>     difficult to extract good conclusions about them. Also, I was thinking in more complicated models, like a Vector
>     Autorregression. Even if the data is stationary, it is quite difficult to extract draws from a conjugate prior
>     (multivariate normal distribution and an Inverse Wishart) such that the draw leads to a stationary path. I think
>     that Gary Koop has a paper on that (he finds the rejection rate to be around 90% or more in a standard Gibbs
>     Sampler, such that he discards any draw that does not lead to a stationary process).   But this is just given my
>     (little) experience :D
>
>         The stationary constraints above are captured by:
>
>             real <lower=-1,upper=1> phi2;
>             real <upper=(1 - fabs(phi2))> phi1;
>
>         The constraint on phi2 is the one you pointed out.  The
>         second pair of equations can be rewritten as:
>
>              phi1 < 1 - phi2
>              phi1 < 1 + phi2
>
>         and we know
>
>             1 - abs(phi2) = min(1 - phi2, 1 + phi2).
>
>
>     I didn't know that we can use parameters in the bounds!! This is very good news!.
>
>           But if you really want to do this, one approach is to parameterize in terms of the roots and then define the
>         coefficients as transformed parameters. But if you want priors on the coefficients instead of the roots, you
>         need to add the log absolute determinant of the Jacobian to lp__. Both of these calcs gets messy at higher orders.
>
>
>     Mmmm, I will probably have to think of this. If there are one-to-one mappings from the roots to the coefficients,
>     should not be any problem, but I am not sure about that. In any case, as you said, I guess that the calcs will get
>     terribly difficult...
>
>     Or maybe priors on the roots will suggest different models?
>     What Andrew calls the Pinocchio Principle.
>     On Thursday, March 7, 2013 2:19:55 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>         On 3/7/13 1:49 PM, Carlos wrote:> Hi all!
>           >
>           > I was wondering if it is possible to constrain the parameters when there
>           > are several restrictions that should be met.
>           > For example, in a stationary autoregressive process of order 2,
>           >
>           >   Y(t) =phi1*Y(t-1) + phi2*(t-2)
>           >
>           > we want that,
>           >
>           >   phi1 + phi2 < 1
>           >   phi1 - phi2< 1
>           >   abs(phi2) < 1
>
>         Funny you should bring that up.  I've had it on our
>         to-do list for a while.  When I asked Andrew, he suggested
>         it would be a bad idea to do this because the data should
>         constrain the solution to be stationary if the process
>         actually is stationary and you don't want to constrain it to
>         a stationary solution if it's not.
>
>         I'm more interested in what you can do with Stan, not whether
>         it's a good idea statistically :-)
>
>         The stationary constraints above are captured by:
>
>             real <lower=-1,upper=1> phi2;
>             real <upper=(1 - fabs(phi2))> phi1;
>
>         The constraint on phi2 is the one you pointed out.  The
>         second pair of equations can be rewritten as:
>
>              phi1 < 1 - phi2
>              phi1 < 1 + phi2
>
>         and we know
>
>             1 - abs(phi2) = min(1 - phi2, 1 + phi2).
>
>           > More generally, if we have an autoregressive process of order P,
>           > is it possible to restrict the roots of the p-th order
>           > polynomial to lie outside the unit circle?
>
>         But if you really want to do this, one approach is to
>         parameterize in terms of the roots and then define the
>         coefficients as transformed parameters.
>
>         But if you want priors on the coefficients
>         instead of the roots, you need to add the log absolute determinant
>         of the Jacobian to lp__.
>
>         Both of these calcs gets messy at higher orders.
>
>         Or maybe priors on the roots will suggest different models?
>         What Andrew calls the Pinocchio Principle.
>
>         - Bob
>
