On Saturday, March 23, 2013 5:46:11 AM UTC-4, Sebastian Weber wrote:
> I just discovered the optimizing method in rstan. It sounds very useful, but the example stated in the help file does not run and it is not obvious to me how to use it. So, what should the example should have been supposed to be?

There were a couple of issues that will get worked out for the next release. Here is a replication from example(glm):

# example from glm()
utils::data(anorexia, package = "MASS")
anorex.1 <- glm(Postwt ~ Prewt + Treat + Prewt,
                family = gaussian, data = anorexia)

# extract data
X <- model.matrix(anorex.1)
y <- anorexia$Postwt

# write a usual stan model
model_code <-
"
data {
  int<lower=1> K;
  int<lower=K> N;
  matrix[N,K]  X;
  vector[N]    y;
}
parameters {
  vector[K] beta;
  real<lower=0> sigma;
}
model {
  y ~ normal(X * beta, sigma);
}
"

# compile
fit <- stan(model_code = model_code, chains = 0,
            data = list(K = ncol(X), N = nrow(X), X = X, y = y))

# wrappers for log-likelihood and gradient
fn <- function(par) log_prob(fit, par)
gr <- function(par) grad_log_prob(fit, par)

# optimize
opt <- optim(c(coef(anorex.1), log_sigma = 2), fn, gr,
             method = "BFGS", control = list(fnscale = -1), hessian = TRUE)
print(opt)

Ben

