Hi guys,

I am wondering how STAN deals with a non-linear model like a Stochastic Volatility (SV) one. I attach here a stan file that describes an AR(2) process with SV (and other exogenous regressors), where the error term of the regression is parametrized as in Kim, Sephard and Chib (1998), that is,

exp(h(t)/2)*e(t),e(t)~N(0,1)

The code compiles, and runs. However, I do not know if I am taking a too naive approach here and if the results that I am getting are not proper... SV models are tricky, and usually people (including myself) focus on the approximations derived in Kim et al's paper, or, more recently, using particle filters. Is there any reason why it should not work in STAN? If it works, I think this is a great improvement...

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Fri Mar 29 15:12:56 2013
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Fri, 29 Mar 2013 15:12:56 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] mixture model problem
Message-ID: <d1zXwYVjuHA.rOuCr9-vD6UJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <d1zXwYVjuHA.kSJIIIsBvZwJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <d1zXwYVjuHA.kSJIIIsBvZwJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/d1zXwYVjuHA/m/rOuCr9-vD6UJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The combination of this parameter declaration:

 > real<lower=7,upper=19> mu[K]; // locations of mixture components

and these priors:

 > mu[1] ~ normal(M[1],SD[1])  T[7,mu[2]-1.5];
 > mu[2] ~ normal(M[2],SD[2])  T[,mu[3]-1];
 > mu[3] ~ normal(M[3],SD[3])  T[,19];

is going to be problematic.  The support of the sampling
distribution for any variable should match the constraints
in its declaration.  If you don't initialize each mu[k]
in the truncated support, Stan can never move.  But even if
you do, you will run into significant rejection.

But I don't think you want a prior or structure in the model
to keep the means separated --- the data should do that.

Your real problem, though, is the data declaration

 > real y[N]; // observations

and sampling loop:

 > for (n in 1:N) {
 >   for (k in 1:K) {
 >     y[n] ~ normal(mu[k],sigma[k]);

This samples y[n] from each of the mu[k].  You need
to code as in this model:

https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/basic_estimators/normal_mixture.stan

Basically, just get rid of the y[n] ~ sampling statement.
The sampling probability for y is implicit in the log_sum_exp
statement.

- Bob

On 3/29/13 4:47 AM, Bets wrote:
